  "layering": {
    "question": "What carries a COM call, and what joins its two ends: FX-COM-001's exchange under one lab session collecting COM-Perf beside Microsoft-Windows-RPC, requested as the product's profiles request it, each COM call set beside the RPC calls of its process and each client call beside the server call carrying its activity id.",
    "answer": "In four runs - 64 calls paced, 256 and 1,024 unpaced, and four bursts of 128 - nothing lost, every COM call was carried by exactly one RPC call at each end, on its interface with its method as the procedure, over local RPC: at the client the RPC call of its activity id, on its thread, starting inside it and stopping 0.6 to 14.5 microseconds after it, its stop sharing the COM stop's activity; at the server the RPC call around it on its thread, with an activity of its own. The server's COM call carried the client's activity id: exactly one server call for each client call, naming the client as the client named the server, on its interface and method, inside the client's call. Every invoke pair by causality and rank was the pair the activity links, and every client RPC call carrying a COM call was so linked to the RPC call that served it, none twice. The client's other RPC calls, 16 a run, all came before its first COM call, and every RPC server call of the server carried one. A call COM answered E_NOINTERFACE stopped with RPC status 0.",
